record("ClaudeLucy-720728f7-c3d1-4be8-9d15-ded6233a1eda-note-2", "{\"id\":\"ClaudeLucy-720728f7-c3d1-4be8-9d15-ded6233a1eda-note-2\",\"speaker\":\"Lucy\",\"text\":\"On 2026-10-19 dhilipsiva pushed three commits to the NixOS peer: b39b78d (\\\"desktop: integrate on-demand Lucy with private shared memory\\\"), 718d749 (\\\"lucy: pin verified Cursor context handoff\\\") and 1b41801 (\\\"update lucy\\\", which published the registration edits I had left in that checkout). The first two change no Lucy memory file; what is new is what they describe, in the README section \\\"Lucy on demand\\\" and in DEPLOYMENT.md: an Ask Lucy GTK chat on his desktop (Super+Alt+L, Fuzzel, `lucy ui`, `lucy chat`); a private user service that keeps complete invoked messages under ~/.local/share/lucy, outside Git and the Nix store; app hooks and adapters (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, VS Code, Zed, Chrome/Firefox, Dolphin, terminals) that activate me only on an explicit address such as \\\"Lucy, …\\\" or \\\"@lucy\\\"; and local inference through Ollama with Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, no cloud fallback. The reusable code is Nibli's lucy-desktop branch at 0f3a317, not merged to main. The packaged identity the desktop model reads is the Nibli CLI constitution template plus the NixOS peer's and Nibli's direct memory files at the pinned revision, so a direct fact reaches it only after a re-pin and a staged generation. He validated the build, the model download and one real local chat on 2026-10-19; staging, reboot and physical acceptance were still pending when I read it, so this is intended behaviour, not a live system, and that private desktop archive is not a peer.\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-10-18T20:06:34Z\",\"host\":\"dhilipsiva-desktop\",\"source\":\"claude-code\",\"session\":\"720728f7-c3d1-4be8-9d15-ded6233a1eda\",\"channel\":\"memory\",\"about\":[\"lucy\",\"nixos\",\"desktop\",\"peer-memory\"],\"private\":false,\"from\":null,\"kr\":null}", Note, Json).
